ice and reboot protocol, with no residue attributable to unrelated exploration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24425d03e5e54765","cmd-09ee26f457014767","cmd-0aaa097755e94ef5","cmd-f78dc7cf66ad4c02","cmd-3e73fdada1ad489b","cmd-ec3493325fb844d8","cmd-d9d429921cef4b59","cmd-5b38e01e3ae24fd6","cmd-b54777ea000446b8","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task are directly and coherently demonstrated: baseline 3-node streaming replication (node1 primary, node2/node3 standbys), a bounded simulated primary-loss with node2 promoted to writable primary without losing any committed row, node3 re-pointed to the new primary across a verified timeline switch, node1 rejoining as a standby of node2 with no data loss, and a fresh post-recovery write propagating cluster-wide. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also executed on all three nodes with pre/post boot-identity, acknowledgement, and full service/data/replication recovery evidence, cross-checked against the fixed after_executor snapshot. No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were identified; the handful of failed commands were normal troubleshooting quickly corrected.","limitations":["Evidence relies on the executor's own captured command outputs rather than independent verification tooling, though outputs are direct psql/systemctl/log output rather than executor narrative.","The two-node availability during node2's restart (quorum) is inferred from pg_stat_replication showing node1/node3 reconnect quickly, not from a continuous liveness probe during the exact restart window.","Long-term persistence of postgresql.auto.conf settings across further reboots beyond the single verified cycle per node was not separately tested."]},"ttft